Here's the complete migration from ASP.NET Web Forms to ASP.NET Core 8.

## Migrated Code

```csharp
// ASP.NET Core 8 — ReportController.cs
[ApiController]
[Route("[controller]")]
public class ReportController : ControllerBase
{
    private readonly ILogger<ReportController> _logger;

    public ReportController(ILogger<ReportController> logger)
    {
        _logger = logger;
    }

    [HttpPost("export")]
    public IActionResult Export()
    {
        var data = GenerateReport();
        return File(
            Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(data),
            "text/csv",
            "report.csv");
    }

    [HttpPost("redirect")]
    public IActionResult RedirectAction()
    {
        if (!User.Identity?.IsAuthenticated ?? true)
        {
            return Redirect("/login");
        }

        try
        {
            ProcessRequest();
            return Redirect("/success");
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            _logger.LogError(ex, "Request processing failed");
            return Redirect("/error");
        }
    }
}
```

## What Changed and Why

### 1. `Response.End()` → just return
`Response.End()` internally called `Thread.Abort()` in .NET Framework. In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`. In ASP.NET Core, returning from the action method ends the response — no explicit termination needed. `File(...)` handles `Content-Type`, `Content-Disposition`, and the response body in one call.

### 2. `Response.Redirect(url, true)` → `return Redirect(url)`
The `true` (endResponse) parameter triggered `Thread.Abort` to prevent code after the redirect from executing. In ASP.NET Core, `Redirect()` returns an `IActionResult` — the `return` keyword naturally stops execution. No abort, no exception.

### 3. `catch (ThreadAbortException)` → removed entirely
This catch block only existed to swallow the exception that `Response.Redirect(url, true)` threw on purpose. With `return Redirect(...)`, there's no exception to catch. **Remove all `ThreadAbortException` catch blocks** — the type doesn't even exist as a thrown exception in modern .NET.

### 4. `Response.Clear/Write/AddHeader` → `File()` result
ASP.NET Core uses the result object pattern instead of mutating the response stream directly.

| .NET Framework | ASP.NET Core 8 |
|---|---|
| `Response.Clear()` | Not needed |
| `Response.ContentType = ...` | Handled by `File()` |
| `Response.AddHeader(...)` | Handled by `File()` |
| `Response.Write(data)` | Handled by `File()` |
| `Response.End()` | Just `return` |

## Common Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Solution |
|---|---|
| Forgetting `return` before `Redirect()` — code after it still executes | Always `return Redirect(...)`. Unlike Framework, nothing stops execution automatically. |
| `User.Identity` can be `null` in Core | Use null-conditional: `User.Identity?.IsAuthenticated ?? false` |
| Catching `OperationCanceledException` and swallowing it | Let it propagate. Use `HttpContext.RequestAborted` for long-running work instead. |
| Still using `HttpContext.Response.End()` | This API doesn't exist in ASP.NET Core. Return an `IActionResult`. |
| Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource` in timeout scenarios | Wrap in `using` if you create one for long-running report generation. |

For long-running report generation, accept the framework's cancellation token:

```csharp
[HttpPost("export")]
public async Task<IActionResult> Export(CancellationToken cancellationToken)
{
    // HttpContext.RequestAborted is automatically wired to this token
    var data = await GenerateReportAsync(cancellationToken);
    return File(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(data), "text/csv", "report.csv");
}
```

This ensures the work stops if the client disconnects — the cooperative replacement for the old preemptive `Thread.Abort` model.
